  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef rl_expected_sarsa(q_values: list, state: int, action: int, reward: float, next_state: int, epsilon: float = 0.1, gamma: float = 0.9, lr: float = 0.1) -> list:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Expected SARSA** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef rl_expected_sarsa(q_values: list, state: int, action: int, reward: float, next_state: int, epsilon: float = 0.1, gamma: float = 0.9, lr: float = 0.1) -> list:\n    Q = np.array(q_values, dtype=np.float64)\n    nA = Q.shape[1]\n    probs = np.full(nA, epsilon / nA)\n    probs[np.argmax(Q[next_state])] += (1.0 - epsilon)\n    exp_q = np.sum(probs * Q[next_state])\n    Q[state, action] += lr * (reward + gamma * exp_q - Q[state, action])\n    return np.round(Q, 6).tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
